          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with a low anal canal malignancy presents with a palpable lump in the groin. Which cancer type and lymphatic drainage pattern best explains this presentation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adenocarcinoma draining to internal iliac nodes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adenocarcinoma arises above the dentate line. While it does drain to internal iliac and mesenteric nodes, it would not present with a groin lump.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Squamous cell carcinoma draining to superficial inguinal nodes</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Below the dentate line, the epithelium is squamous (producing squamous cell carcinoma) and lymphatics drain to the superficial inguinal nodes — directly explaining the groin swelling.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adenocarcinoma draining to superficial inguinal nodes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adenocarcinoma arises from columnar epithelium above the dentate line, where lymph drains to pelvic and mesenteric nodes, not to the inguinal region.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Squamous cell carcinoma draining to internal iliac nodes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Squamous carcinoma does arise below the dentate line, but lymph from this region drains to the superficial inguinal nodes, not the internal iliac nodes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the dentate line anatomy: below this line, the lining is squamous (so cancers are squamous cell carcinomas) and lymphatics drain to the superficial inguinal nodes — explaining a groin lump with low anal cancer."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The dentate line predicts both tumour histology and lymphatic drainage. Below it, squamous epithelium gives rise to squamous cell carcinoma, and lymph drains to the superficial inguinal nodes."
                }
              ],
