          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>An anal fissure is found at a lateral position rather than the posterior midline</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An anal fissure is found at a lateral position rather than the posterior midline. Which of the following should this finding raise suspicion for?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Grade III internal haemorrhoids</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Haemorrhoids present with painless bleeding or prolapse, not as a mucosal tear. Their position is defined by the anal cushion locations, not by midline versus lateral.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pilonidal sinus disease</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A pilonidal sinus is a hair-containing sinus of the natal cleft — a separate condition entirely unrelated to the position of an anal fissure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Crohn's disease, tuberculosis, or malignancy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Typical anal fissures occur at the posterior midline due to poor blood supply and maximal mechanical stress. A lateral or atypical fissure is a red flag for an underlying cause such as Crohn's disease, tuberculosis, malignancy, or HIV.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Perianal abscess</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A perianal abscess presents as a hot, tender swelling with fever, arising from infected cryptoglandular glands. It is a separate condition and would not be diagnosed by fissure location.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A lateral or atypical anal fissure is a clinical red flag. Typical fissures form at the posterior midline; when a fissure appears elsewhere, suspect Crohn's disease, tuberculosis, malignancy, or HIV."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! A fissure away from the posterior midline should always prompt investigation for systemic or inflammatory causes such as Crohn's disease, tuberculosis, malignancy, or HIV."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
