          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with a chronic anal fissure has not responded to stool softeners and t</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with a chronic anal fissure has not responded to stool softeners and topical glyceryl trinitrate. What is the next step in management?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rubber-band ligation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Rubber-band ligation is a treatment for Grade I–II internal haemorrhoids, not anal fissure. It does not address the internal sphincter spasm that perpetuates a chronic fissure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immediate lateral internal sphincterotomy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Sphincterotomy is effective but reserved for truly refractory cases. The management ladder requires botulinum toxin injection as an intermediate step before surgical intervention.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Incision and drainage</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incision and drainage is the treatment for a perianal abscess. An anal fissure is a mucosal tear, not a collection of pus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Botulinum toxin injection</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Botulinum toxin chemically relaxes the internal sphincter, reducing the spasm that prevents fissure healing. It is the next step before considering surgical sphincterotomy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The management ladder for chronic anal fissure is: stool softeners → topical GTN or diltiazem → botulinum toxin injection → lateral internal sphincterotomy for refractory cases. Every step treats proportionately and aims to protect continence."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The fissure management ladder progresses: stool softeners → topical GTN/diltiazem → botulinum toxin → lateral internal sphincterotomy. Each step escalates proportionately while protecting the sphincter."
                }
              ],
